Given the system of logarithmic equations: \begin{align*}
\log x - \log y &= a, \\
\log y - \log z &= 15, \text{ and} \\
\log z - \log x &= -7.
\end{align*} We can express these equations using properties of logarithms. First, we can combine the logarithms in each equation: \begin{align*}
\log \left( \frac{x}{y} \right) &= a, \\
\log \left( \frac{y}{z} \right) &= 15, \\
\log \left( \frac{z}{x} \right) &= -7.
\end{align*} Now, we can multiply the first equation by $\log x$, the second equation by $\log y$, and the third equation by $\log z$ and then get $\log x$, $\log y$, and $\log z$ in terms of $a$. By doing this, we will create a system of linear equations. Notice that: \begin{align*}
\log x \cdot \log \left( \frac{x}{y} \right) &= \log x \cdot a, \\
\log y \cdot \log \left( \frac{y}{z} \right) &= \log y \cdot 15, \\
\log z \cdot \log \left( \frac{z}{x} \right) &= \log z \cdot (-7).
\end{align*} This simplifies to: \begin{align*}
\log x \cdot \log x - \log x \cdot \log y &= \log x \cdot a, \\
\log y \cdot \log y - \log y \cdot \log z &= \log y \cdot 15, \\
\log z \cdot \log z - \log z \cdot \log x &= \log z \cdot (-7).
\end{align*} We can rewrite these as: \begin{align*}
(\log x)^2 - \log x \cdot \log y &= a \cdot \log x, \\
(\log y)^2 - \log y \cdot \log z &= 15 \cdot \log y, \\
(\log z)^2 - \log z \cdot \log x &= -7 \cdot \log z.
\end{align*} By adding all three equations, we get: \begin{align*}
(\log x)^2 - \log x \cdot \log y + (\log y)^2 - \log y \cdot \log z + (\log z)^2 - \log z \cdot \log x &= a \cdot \log x + 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z.
\end{align*} Notice that we can factor the left side as: \begin{align*}
(\log x)^2 + (\log y)^2 + (\log z)^2 - \log x \cdot \log y - \log y \cdot \log z - \log z \cdot \log x &= a \cdot \log x + 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z.
\end{align*} We can complete the square for $\log x$, $\log y$, and $\log z$: \begin{align*}
(\log x + \log y + \log z)^2 - 2 \log x \cdot \log y - 2 \log y \cdot \log z - 2 \log z \cdot \log x &= a \cdot \log x + 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z.
\end{align*} We can combine terms to get: \begin{align*}
(\log x + \log y + \log z)^2 - (\log x \cdot \log y + \log y \cdot \log z + \log z \cdot \log x) &= a \cdot \log x + 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z.
\end{align*} We can substitute the original equations: \begin{align*}
(\log x + \log y + \log z)^2 - (\log x \cdot \log x + 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z) &= a \cdot \log x + 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z.
\end{align*} By rearranging, we get: \begin{align*}
(\log x + \log y + \log z)^2 - a \cdot \log x &= 15 \cdot \log y - 7 \cdot \log z + \log x \cdot \log x.
\end{align*

